
 #  Joins
    Given orders(id, customer_id, amount) and customers(id, name):

    Query and What it does :-

    Only rows with a match in both tables ->
    SELECT o.*, c.name FROM orders o INNER JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;

    All orders, even if the customer is missing (nulls for unmatched) ->
    SELECT o.*, c.name FROM orders o LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;

    All rows from the right table, matched where possible
    (rarely used — usually rewritten as a LEFT JOIN by swapping table order) ->
    SELECT c.*, o.* FROM customers c RIGHT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id;

    All rows from both sides, matched where possible
    (not supported directly in MySQL — emulate with UNION of LEFT and RIGHT joins)->

    Postgres / SQL Server / Oracle (has FULL OUTER JOIN natively)
    SELECT o.id AS order_id, o.amount, c.id AS customer_id, c.name
    FROM orders o
    FULL OUTER JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;

    MySQL (no FULL OUTER JOIN — build it from LEFT + RIGHT + UNION):
    SELECT o.id AS order_id, o.amount, c.id AS customer_id, c.name
    FROM orders o
    LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id

    UNION

    SELECT o.id AS order_id, o.amount, c.id AS customer_id, c.name
    FROM orders o
    RIGHT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;

    Table joined to itself — e.g., employee-to-manager ->
    SELECT a.id, b.id FROM employees a, employees b WHERE a.manager_id = b.id; (or SELF JOIN)

    Every combination of rows from both tables
    (Cartesian product) — rare, watch for accidental cross joins from missing ON clauses ->
    SELECT * FROM a CROSS JOIN b;

    Classic "anti-join" pattern ->
    Find customers with no orders: SELECT c.* FROM customers c LEFT JOIN
    orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.id IS NULL;

